    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114336,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 577,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:18:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 748,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 578,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114337,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 578,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DA05.0 Developmental odontogenic cysts</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:18:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 749,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 579,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114338,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 579,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> orthokeratinized variant of odontogenic keratocyst.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:18:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 750,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 580,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114339,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:18:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114340,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "<p>Most OOCs (80%) are found in the mandible and 65% are found in the posterior body and ramus region {6166916; 20121617; 21062939}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-01-10T12:34:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114341,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>OOC usually presents as a painless swelling {20121617; 21062939}, but many are incidental findings during radiographic examination. Rare cases of multiple or bilateral OOC have been reported, but there is no evidence of any association with naevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome (Gorlin syndrome) {24737103; 31119532}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>OOCs are well-demarcated spherical, unilocular, radiolucent lesions, often with a corticated margin. Occasional cases (&lt;&nbsp;10%) are multilocular {21062939}. As many as 70% of all lesions are associated with an impacted tooth, with a radiological similarity to dentigerous cyst {20121617}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:18:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114342,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>OOC is rare and accounts for &le;&nbsp;1% of odontogenic cysts {32868122; 30758760}. It arises over a wide age range, with an average of about 35&nbsp;years and a peak in the third and fourth decades of life. About 65% of patients are male {6166916; 20121617; 21062939}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:18:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114343,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:18:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114344,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>OOC is a developmental cyst that most likely arises from remnants of dental lamina {9568510}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:18:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114345,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "<p>Multiple fragments of cyst wall. The contents are cream or yellow in colour, with a cheesy or buttery texture.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-10T16:50:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114346,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>OOC is lined by thin regular orthokeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with a prominent granular cell layer and inconspicuous or low cuboidal basal cells. It is heavily keratinized, often with lamellae of keratin filling the lumen. The wall is fibrous and may show areas of inflammation. There may be parakeratinized or non-keratinized areas, but these form a small part of the lining and are often associated with inflammation {6166916}. Rare examples include sebaceous glands in the lining {18022486}. Microcysts may be seen in the wall in 5% of cases {6166916; 9568510}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:18:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114347,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 131,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Examination of an aspirate may show lamellae and fragments of orthokeratin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:18:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 758,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 588,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114348,
